(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-histology-quiz"]={"title":"Histology — Anatomy Exam 1","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What are the four primary tissue types in the human body, and their primary function each?","choices":["Epithelial (protection), connective (support), nerve (control), muscle (movement)","Epithelial (movement), connective (control), nerve (support), muscle (protection)","Only three tissue types exist: epithelial, connective, and muscle","Epithelial (support), connective (protection), nerve (movement), muscle (control)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The four tissue types are epithelial (primarily protection), connective (primarily support), nerve (primarily control), and muscle (primarily movement). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a defining structural feature of epithelial tissue regarding its composition and vascularity?","choices":["Consists almost entirely of cells with little extracellular material, and is avascular","Composed of roughly equal proportions of cells and extracellular matrix, and lacks blood vessels","Composed mostly of extracellular matrix with relatively few cells, and is richly supplied with blood vessels","Composed almost entirely of cells, and is richly supplied with blood vessels throughout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epithelial tissue consists almost entirely of cells with little extracellular material, and epithelial cells are avascular. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the basement membrane, and what is it composed of?","choices":["A thin layer of muscle tissue positioned directly beneath the epithelium","A richly vascular structure that supplies nutrients directly to the overlying epithelial cells","A nonliving adhesive substance secreted by epithelial cells, composed of connective tissue (collagen and glycoproteins)","A living cellular layer secreted and maintained by connective tissue cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"The basement membrane is a nonliving adhesive substance secreted by epithelial cells, similar to adhesive tape, composed of connective tissue elements (collagen and glycoproteins). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of epithelial tissue?","choices":["Secretes serous fluids to lubricate structures","Transmits nerve impulses to targets","Stores calcium and phosphate ions","Contracts its fibers in order to generate body movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epithelial tissue secretes serous fluids to lubricate structures, in addition to providing protection, filtering, secreting, and reabsorbing materials. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How is epithelial tissue classified by cell shape?","choices":["Areolar, adipose, and reticular tissue types, which are subtypes of connective tissue","Simple, stratified, and transitional layering only","Squamous (flat), cuboidal (as tall as wide), and columnar (tall and column-shaped)","Hyaline, fibrocartilage, and elastic cartilage types"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epithelial tissue is classified by cell shape into squamous (flat, \"scale\"-shaped), cuboidal (as tall as they are wide), and columnar (tall and column-shaped) cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the difference between \"simple\" and \"stratified\" epithelium?","choices":["Both terms describe the identical number of layers","Simple and stratified describe cell shape, not layer count","Simple has a single layer of cells; stratified has multiple or stacked layers","Simple has multiple layers; stratified has one layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Simple epithelium has a single layer of cells, while stratified epithelium has multiple or stacked layers. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is simple squamous epithelium found, and what is its function?","choices":["Used for absorption — found lining the walls of the digestive tract","Permeable, used for filtration and exchange — found in capillaries, alveoli, and kidney glomeruli","Used for protection — found lining the skin surface and the inside of the mouth","Used for secretion of enzymes — found lining the ducts of the salivary glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Simple squamous epithelium is permeable and used for filtration and exchange, found in locations like capillaries, alveoli, and kidney glomeruli. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is stratified squamous epithelium found, and what is its function?","choices":["Used for filtration — found in capillaries and kidney glomeruli","Used for reabsorption — found in kidney tubules","Used for protection — found in the skin and inside of the mouth","Used for movement — found in skeletal muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stratified squamous epithelium is used for protection, found in locations like the skin (keratinized) and the inside of the mouth. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Simple cuboidal epithelium is described as roughly cube-shaped and one cell thick. Where is it found?","choices":["Forming the multiple layers of the epidermis of the skin","Lining the mouth, throat, and esophagus as a lining","Lining the walls of blood vessels only, as seen in capillaries","Lining ducts in kidneys, where reabsorption and secretory activities take place"],"answer":3,"exp":"Simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts in kidneys and other locations where reabsorption and secretory activities take place. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is pseudostratified columnar epithelium, and where is it found?","choices":["A true multi-layered stratified tissue found only within the epidermis of the skin","A squamous-type tissue found lining the thin walls of capillaries and pulmonary alveoli","A cuboidal-type tissue found lining the collecting tubules of the kidney nephron","Gives the appearance of more than one layer of columnar cells despite being simple; found lining the nasal cavity, sinuses, and trachea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium gives the appearance of more than one layer of columnar epithelial cells (despite being simple), and lines locations like the nasal cavity, sinuses, auditory tubes, trachea, and bronchi. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where is transitional epithelium found, and what is distinctive about its surface cells?","choices":["Cube-shaped cells at the surface; found lining the collecting ducts of the kidney","Column-shaped cells at the surface; found lining the wall of the digestive tract","Flat surface cells; found lining the thin walls of capillaries and alveoli","Dome-shaped surface cells; found lining the bladder, ureters, and superior urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Transitional epithelium has dome-shaped surface cells and is found lining the bladder, ureters, and superior urethra. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What are the three general categories of \"living\" cells found in connective tissue, based on suffix naming?","choices":["Macrophages, neurons, and chondrocytes","Only \"cyte\" cells exist in connective tissue","\"Blast\" cells (remove), \"cyte\" cells (create), and \"clast\" cells (maintain)","\"Blast\" cells (create), \"cyte\" cells (maintain), and \"clast\" cells (remove)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Connective tissue's living cellular components include \"blast\" cells (create), \"cyte\" cells (maintain), and \"clast\" cells (remove), along with macrophages, white blood cells, mast cells, and adipose cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of connective tissue?","choices":["Contracts its fibers to generate voluntary body movement","Generates nerve impulses and transmits them along its length","Connects, binds, and supports structures (e.g., tendons, ligaments)","Filters and secretes materials as a lining"],"answer":2,"exp":"Connective tissue connects, binds, and supports structures such as tendons and ligaments, in addition to protecting/cushioning organs, insulating, and transporting substances like blood. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the \"matrix\" of connective tissue, and what are its three components?","choices":["The nonliving component, consisting of ground substance, fluid, and fibers","A structure unique to bone tissue only, absent from all other connective tissue types","A membrane-like structure found exclusively within epithelial tissue, not connective tissue","The living cellular portion of connective tissue, made up of blast, cyte, and clast cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"The matrix is the \"non-living\" component of connective tissue, consisting of ground substance (proteoglycan aggregates, glycosaminoglycans, hyaluronic acid), fluid (containing gases and nutrients, plus minerals), and fibers (collagen, elastin, and reticular fibers). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is collagen, and what is a notable property of it?","choices":["A form of ground substance that lacks any meaningful structural strength","A mineral component of the connective tissue matrix, similar to calcium salts","A specialized cell type found only within the matrix of bone tissue","A fibrous protein constituting about 25% of the weight of most mammals, with high tensile strength"],"answer":3,"exp":"Collagen is a fibrous protein present in connective tissue structure (derived from a Greek word meaning \"to glue together\"), constituting about 25% of the weight of most mammals, and has high tensile strength (a 4.5 pound load is needed to break a collagen fiber 1 mm thick). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are reticular fibers, structurally?","choices":["Mineral deposits found specifically within the calcified matrix of bone","Thick elastin fibers with pronounced spring-like recoil properties","Very fine collagen fibers that branch out to form a network, filling space between tissues and organs","Cellular structures found uniquely within epithelial tissue linings, not connective tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reticular fibers are actually very fine collagen fibers that branch out to form a network, filling the space between other tissues and organs. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are elastic fibers, and what property do they have?","choices":["Rigid mineral crystals embedded permanently within the bone matrix","Fibers containing the protein elastin, with spring-like properties that can be compressed or distended","Fine collagen fibers forming a delicate, loosely branching network","Cellular fibers found only within the fascicles of muscle tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elastic fibers contain the protein elastin and have spring-like properties, able to be compressed or distended. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is areolar (\"loose connective\") tissue, and what is one of its functions?","choices":["A mineral-rich tissue found only within the calcified matrix of compact bone","A dense, tightly packed fiber tissue found only within tendons and ligaments","An avascular tissue type found only within the matrix of hyaline cartilage structures","Serves as \"packing material\" with elastic and collagen fibers, and separates muscles to allow them to slide over each other"],"answer":3,"exp":"Areolar tissue is a loose connective tissue serving as \"packing material\" with elastic and collagen fibers; it forms a basal membrane component and separates muscles, allowing them to slide over each other. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is distinctive about adipose tissue's cellular composition, and what are its functions?","choices":["Cells account for only 10% of tissue mass, mostly matrix; functions primarily in mineral storage","Cells account for 90% of tissue mass (little matrix present), engorged with lipids; functions as insulator, shock absorber, and energy store","Consists mostly of collagen fibers with few cells; functions primarily in blood cell production","Highly avascular tissue with a notably slow healing rate; its main function is body movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Adipose tissue is highly vascular, with cells accounting for 90% of tissue mass (little matrix present) and engorged with lipids; it functions as an insulator, shock absorber, and energy store. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What distinguishes dense regular connective tissue from dense irregular connective tissue?","choices":["Both describe the identical parallel fiber arrangement seen only in tendons, with no meaningful structural difference between the two categories at all","Dense regular is found only in cartilage matrix; dense irregular is found only in compact bone tissue, with each restricted entirely to that one tissue type","Dense regular has closely packed parallel collagen fibers (tendons, ligaments) where tension runs one direction; dense irregular has closely packed non-directional collagen forming sheets (dermis, fascia) where tension runs many directions","Dense regular has non-directional collagen forming sheets (dermis, fascia) where tension runs many directions; dense irregular has parallel collagen fibers (tendons, ligaments) where tension runs one direction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dense regular connective tissue has closely packed parallel collagen fibers, found where tension is exerted in a particular direction (tendons, ligaments), while dense irregular connective tissue has closely packed non-directional collagen forming sheets where tension is exerted in many directions (dermis of skin, muscle fascia, organ and nerve coverings). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is elastic connective tissue composed of, and what are examples of where it's found?","choices":["Composed predominantly of collagen fibers; typically found within tendons","Composed of mostly elastin fibers; found in vocal cords and ligamenta flava","Composed of ground substance only; found in the membrane","Composed of mineral crystals such as calcium salts; typically found within bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elastic connective tissue is composed of mostly elastin fibers, found in structures like the vocal cords and ligamenta flava (vertebral connective tissue). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"According to the material, what is the most abundant and widely distributed tissue type?","choices":["Nervous tissue","Epithelial tissue","Muscle tissue","Connective tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Connective tissue is described as the most abundant and widely distributed tissue. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What ground substance component is described as a \"slippery polysaccharide\" that reduces friction?","choices":["Calcium hydroxyapatite","Collagen","Hyaluronic acid","Elastin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hyaluronic acid is described as a slippery polysaccharide, part of the ground substance component of the connective tissue matrix. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What do glycosaminoglycans do within the ground substance, per the material?","choices":["Produce the myelin sheaths that wrap around peripheral nerve fibers","Carry a positive charge and actively repel surrounding water molecules","Negatively charged, bind Na+ and K+, and attract water","Form the rigid mineral crystal component embedded within bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glycosaminoglycans are negatively charged, bind Na+ and K+, and attract water (H2O) within the ground substance. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are proteoglycan aggregates described as, structurally, within the ground substance?","choices":["Pine tree-shaped molecules","Flat, scale-shaped structures","Coiled, spring-shaped structures","Branching, network-forming structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Proteoglycan aggregates are described as pine tree-shaped molecules, part of the ground substance. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What cell type contains heparin and histamine, per the material's list of connective tissue cells?","choices":["Macrophages","Mast cells","Adipose cells","Fibroblasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mast cells are described as containing heparin and histamine. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is notable about the regeneration potential and one surface exposure of epithelial cells?","choices":["Exhibits a high capacity for regeneration, with both surfaces embedded within surrounding connective tissue","High regeneration potential, with one side always exposed to the body exterior or an organ cavity","Exhibits a low capacity for regeneration, with one side that is always richly vascularized","Exhibits a low capacity for regeneration, with no surface ever exposed to a cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Epithelial cells have high regeneration potential, and one side is always exposed to either the body exterior or an organ cavity. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of the following describes a listed location of epithelial tissue?","choices":["Covers the body and lines the cavities, tubes, ducts, and blood vessels inside the body","Found exclusively within the muscular walls that line hollow internal organs","Found only within the ventricles of the central nervous system","Found only within the red and yellow marrow cavities of bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epithelial tissue covers the body, lines the cavities, tubes, ducts, and blood vessels inside the body, and covers the organs inside body cavities. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does the \"fluid\" component of the connective tissue matrix contain?","choices":["Gases and nutrients, plus minerals such as calcium salts","Only myelin produced by supporting glial cells of the nervous system","Consists solely of the living blast, cyte, and clast cell types","Consists solely of collagen and elastin protein fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fluid component of the connective tissue matrix contains gases and nutrients, along with minerals such as calcium salts. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Besides mast cells and adipose cells, which other cell types are listed among the living components of connective tissue?","choices":["Squamous and cuboidal cells","Macrophages and white blood cells","Neurons and their glial cells","Only chondrocytes and osteocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Macrophages and white blood cells are listed among the living cellular components of connective tissue, alongside blast/cyte/clast cells, mast cells, and adipose cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
